WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.012 position:50% size:92% align:center
The key-value cache stores earlier tokens’ keys

00:00:04.012 --> 00:00:07.497 position:50% size:92% align:center
and values in device memory. It is not the GPU’s

00:00:07.497 --> 00:00:09.240 position:50% size:92% align:center
L1 or L2 hardware cache.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:12.922 position:50% size:92% align:center
The starter has room for seventeen token slots,

00:00:12.922 --> 00:00:14.763 position:50% size:92% align:center
with four FP32 values per

00:00:14.763 --> 00:00:16.457 position:50% size:92% align:center
key and four per value.

00:00:18.477 --> 00:00:21.451 position:50% size:92% align:center
Four token slots are logically part of the old

00:00:21.451 --> 00:00:24.555 position:50% size:92% align:center
history. Reserving seventeen slots does not mean

00:00:24.555 --> 00:00:26.947 position:50% size:92% align:center
all seventeen belong to that history.

00:00:27.167 --> 00:00:29.809 position:50% size:92% align:center
The kernel’s four threads each inspect one

00:00:29.809 --> 00:00:32.137 position:50% size:92% align:center
component across the four old tokens.

00:00:34.157 --> 00:00:37.238 position:50% size:92% align:center
A key and value together contain eight FP32

00:00:37.238 --> 00:00:38.958 position:50% size:92% align:center
components per token, or

00:00:38.958 --> 00:00:40.749 position:50% size:92% align:center
thirty-two payload bytes.

00:00:40.969 --> 00:00:43.935 position:50% size:92% align:center
Four old tokens therefore supply one hundred and

00:00:43.935 --> 00:00:46.654 position:50% size:92% align:center
twenty-eight requested payload bytes to this

00:00:46.654 --> 00:00:48.199 position:50% size:92% align:center
step. Hardware caches may

00:00:48.199 --> 00:00:49.929 position:50% size:92% align:center
affect the physical traffic.

00:00:51.949 --> 00:00:54.709 position:50% size:92% align:center
The new key and value are written at token slot

00:00:54.709 --> 00:00:57.411 position:50% size:92% align:center
four. That is thirty-two append payload bytes.

00:00:57.631 --> 00:01:00.359 position:50% size:92% align:center
Reading old history and appending a new entry

00:01:00.359 --> 00:01:01.814 position:50% size:92% align:center
are different jobs, with

00:01:01.814 --> 00:01:03.391 position:50% size:92% align:center
different amounts of data.

00:01:05.411 --> 00:01:07.944 position:50% size:92% align:center
Consider eight old tokens with the same

00:01:07.944 --> 00:01:10.803 position:50% size:92% align:center
component width and one new token. Pause and

00:01:10.803 --> 00:01:13.791 position:50% size:92% align:center
predict which payload doubles: the old-history

00:01:13.791 --> 00:01:15.480 position:50% size:92% align:center
read, the append, or both?

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:17.267 position:50% size:92% align:center
The reserved capacity still

00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:18.601 position:50% size:92% align:center
has room for this case.

00:01:24.421 --> 00:01:27.269 position:50% size:92% align:center
The old-history read becomes two hundred and

00:01:27.269 --> 00:01:30.376 position:50% size:92% align:center
fifty-six payload bytes. The single-token append

00:01:30.376 --> 00:01:31.995 position:50% size:92% align:center
remains thirty-two bytes.

00:01:32.215 --> 00:01:34.997 position:50% size:92% align:center
Longer history changes how much old data is

00:01:34.997 --> 00:01:38.039 position:50% size:92% align:center
consumed, even when the number of new tokens is

00:01:38.039 --> 00:01:39.851 position:50% size:92% align:center
unchanged. These are payload

00:01:39.851 --> 00:01:41.857 position:50% size:92% align:center
counts, not measured bandwidth.
